Z||000000||lews84||12-17-1999||04:02 PM||||I am completely redoing my brakes and I bought Earl's steel braided brake lines. Dumb questions: do the long or the short lines go on the front?<P>I am pretty sure it is the long ones, but I just want to make sure before I screw it up.<P>Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||12-17-1999||07:54 PM||||The longer brake hoses go up front and the shorter ones go in back.||||reg||1
Z||000002||WGraffiKs||01-04-2000||11:39 PM||||So, Lews,<BR>How do the brakes feel with the new lines?<P>I've upgraded the lines on numerous bikes with fantastic results, but have only pondered doing it on my GT, so far.  Any better control feel?  How much $ was the kit?<BR><p>[This message has been edited by WGraffiKs (edited 01-04-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000003||lews84||01-05-2000||10:50 PM||||To be honest, I haven't finished the brakes yet. I haven't even finished the first corner . Lots of excuses, Christmas, it's cold in the garage... Mainly I've been lazy.<P>The hoses came from the Fiero Store. I think they were about $90. About the same price as a complete set of rubber hoses from the same source.<P>I doubt that I will be able to tell if they are any better than the standard hoses because my brakes were really shot (rotors, calipers, and hoses). My Camry had much better brake feel. Since I had to replace the  hoses anyway, I figured I would go with the braided lines for the same price. They where all we ever used when I was racing Formula Fords.||||reg||1
Z||000004||2birds||01-06-2000||08:56 PM||||I used the goodridge braided hoses from the fiero store on mine, and couldn't tell the difference from stock.  At $80 for the complete set, it was still a good price, IMHO, even  if there was no diference in braking.  My $.02||||reg||1
